%N A400058 Decimal expansion of the Shannon entropy of Benford's law, -Sum_{d=1..9} p(d)*log(p(d)), where p(d) = log_10(1+1/d).
%C A400058 Benford's law: the leading decimal digit d = 1..9 of a number has probability p(d) = log_10(1+1/d) (p(1) = A007524). This constant is the Shannon entropy of that distribution, with natural logarithms (nats).
%C A400058 In bits the entropy is (this constant)/log(2) = 2.87591612099013160177... .
%C A400058 See A401525 and A401526 for the entropy of the leading digit of a random integer k in 1..N chosen with probability proportional to 1/k.
%H A400058 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/BenfordsLaw.html">Benford's Law</a>.
%H A400058 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Benford%27s_law">Benford's law</a>.
%F A400058 Equals -Sum_{d=1..9} log_10(1+1/d)*log(log_10(1+1/d)).
%F A400058 Equals log(log(10)) - (1/log(10))*Sum_{d=1..9} log(1+1/d)*log(log(1+1/d)).
%e A400058 1.99343315079120426982520886130540209779551973069135822518707...
%o A400058 (Python) # via mpmath; E = -Sum_{d=1..9} p(d)*log(p(d)), p(d) = log_10(1+1/d)
%o A400058 from mpmath import mp, mpf, log
%o A400058 digits = 105          # number of digits to compute; the only parameter to change
%o A400058 mp.dps = digits + 20  # working precision: 20 guard digits (the sum has only 9 terms)
%o A400058 E = -sum(p*log(p) for p in (log(1 + mpf(1)/d)/log(10) for d in range(1, 10)))
%o A400058 print(", ".join(str(int(E*10**(digits-1)))))  # first `digits` digits of E = 1.9934..., truncated
%Y A400058 Cf. A007524, A213201 (mean of the leading digit under Benford's law), A401525, A401526.
%K A400058 nonn,cons,new
%O A400058 1,2
%A A400058 _Vladimir Khomyakov_, Nov 23 2026
